  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 253 PM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nIt is another afternoon of showers and thunderstorms across Colorado \nas monsoonal moisture continues to raise precipitable water (PW) for \nthe region to values (1.0\"-1.6\") that greatly exceed the 90th \npercentile of climatology. Numerous thunderstorms have already \ndeveloped over the Front Range and the urban corridor, with radar-\nestimated totals with the strongest storms ranging from 1.00\" to 2.0\"\nin one hour. Weak mean cloud layer winds (<10KT) will lead to \nslow storm motions and torrential rainfall that may lead to \nlocalized flash flooding, particularly over burn scars and areas \nwith antecedent soil moisture from Tuesday's storms.\n\nWe have issued a Flood Watch for flash flooding for the Palmer \nDivide, where high-res model guidance has been a bit more bullish \nwith thunderstorm coverage and instability will be more robust \n(MLCAPE ~1000 J/kg). However, localized flash flooding may occur \noutside of the Watch if storm redevelopment occurs over the same \nareas, and with any storms that become anchored to the terrain. \nWe expect showers and storms to start diminishing in coverage by 9\npm, with perhaps a few decaying thunderstorms lingering over the \nmountains and the easternmost Colorado plains.\n\nFor tomorrow, the upper level ridge currently over the southwest \nU.S. weakens, and westerly winds aloft cuts off the flow of \nmonsoonal moisture from the southwest. We expect shower coverage to \ndecrease, although afternoon showers will still be possible (20% to \n35% chance) mainly over the highest peaks, northern foothills, and \nthe Palmer Divide. The northeast Colorado plains will still hold on \nto PWs around 1.5\" and dewpoints in the low 60s, so this is where \nthe better chance for a strong isolated thunderstorm will reside. We \nare not overly concerned about flash flooding for tomorrow due to \nthe decreased rainfall coverage as well as faster storm motions, \nwith cloud layer mean winds around 20KT.\n\nThe passage of a shortwave trough will bring another chance for \nisolated strong to severe thunderstorms on Friday, although \nuncertainty remains regarding the convective setup due to the \nmesoscale convective system (MCS) that model guidance has suggested \nwill track through central Nebraska in the early morning hours, \nwhich could produce an outflow front that greatly alters convective \nparameters for Friday. Nevertheless, decent northwest flow aloft (20-\n30KT) and the advection of higher dewpoints from the central plains \ndue to easterly flow at the surface could lead to a few strong \nstorms that produce erratic gusty outflows up to 50 mph.\n\nLong-range ensembles are in good agreement that the upper level \nridge strengthens to our west on Saturday, the atmosphere dries out, \nand temperatures rise above normal once again. Triple digits and \nheat concerns are becoming increasingly likely for Sunday and \nMonday over the plains, including the Denver metro. Uncertainty \nenters the picture halfway through next week due to a series of \nshortwaves sliding through the Pacific Northwest, and a weakening \nof the ridge over the Four Corners.",
